WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:05.331
We tend to define a movie's success mostly
by how it performs in American theaters, but

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:09.360
big-budget films can live or die by how well
they do overseas.

00:00:09.709 --> 00:00:14.019
Still, while Hollywood is becoming more aware
of global audiences, there are still plenty

00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:17.320
of movies that simply don't go over well in
certain countries.

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:22.020
Consequently, some of your favorite films
have been banned internationally for a variety

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:23.020
of reasons.

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:29.060
From unanticipated cultural insensitivity
to uncomfortable reminders of real-life violence,

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:32.680
here are some films you've probably seen that
were banned around the world.

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:34.940
Kingsman: The Golden Circle

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:39.680
The biggest problem for American audiences
with Kingsman: The Golden Circle was probably

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:41.900
Channing Tatum's minimal screen time.

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:45.500
But the government of Cambodia had a very
different issue.

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:49.850
According to their culture ministry's film
department, the film's use of Cambodia for

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:54.280
the villain's headquarters contributed to
the world's negative impression of Cambodian

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:55.140
organized crime.

00:00:55.620 --> 00:01:01.200
Worse, the villainous hideout closely resembles
Ta Prohm, a well-known Cambodian temple.

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:05.100
The film's setting led to Cambodia completely
banning any screenings.

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:10.080
But considering that the Kingsman series is
a pastiche of James Bond films, you could

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:13.780
say that cultural insensitivity is just part
of the genre.

00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:15.520
Zoolander

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:20.960
Ben Stiller has a knack for playing bizarre
narcissists, and his role as Derek Zoolander

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:23.200
ranks among the most outlandish.

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:28.060
From life-and-death catwalks to Zoolander
getting brainwashed into trying to assassinate

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:32.590
the Prime Minister of Malaysia, the film is
dripping with absurd humor.

00:01:32.590 --> 00:01:37.300
Unfortunately, though unsurprisingly, some
of that humor didn't quite land with the Malaysian

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:39.820
government, which banned the film from release.

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:43.780
It wasn't just the assassination plot that
put Zoolander in hot water, it was the reason

00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:49.299
for it: the prime minister is targeted because
he wants to put an end to Malaysian sweatshops.

00:01:49.299 --> 00:01:54.140
Making light of working conditions was unsurprisingly
offensive to the real Malaysian government,

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:55.520
and the film was promptly banned.

00:01:55.700 --> 00:02:00.220
Malaysia wasn't the only country upset, as
its neighbor Singapore also banned it as well.

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:01.220
"You're dead to me boy."

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:05.340
The Wolf of Wall Street

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:11.000
Martin Scorsese's The Wolf of Wall Street
is packed with drug-filled escapades, explicit

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:13.920
love-making scenes, and colorfully profane
dialogue.

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:21.120
It even holds the record for the most uses
of the f-word in a non-documentary, with 506

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:22.060
instances in total.

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:27.840
It's excessive in the truest sense, and it's
easy to understand how it could possibly offend

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:28.340
audiences.

00:02:28.460 --> 00:02:33.099
It's unsurprising, then, that Wolf of Wall
Street was censored, edited, and banned in

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:34.240
multiple countries.

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:39.620
Nepal and Malaysia banned it outright, while
India, the United Arab Emirates, and Lebanon

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:40.740
all cut scenes.

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:45.239
Singapore restricted showings to less than
a dozen theaters, but that didn't stop it

00:02:45.239 --> 00:02:47.769
from coming in first at their box office.

00:02:47.769 --> 00:02:53.510
Ultimately, the film was a critical and commercial
success, despite, or perhaps because of, the

00:02:53.510 --> 00:02:54.100
controversy.

00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:55.540
"The show goes on!"

00:02:58.180 --> 00:02:59.420
"This is my home!"

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:01.100
Shrek 2

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:05.510
It can be very tricky to translate comedy
into a different language.

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:10.409
You have to match the style and tone of the
original while also adjusting cultural references

00:03:10.409 --> 00:03:13.640
to ones that your new audience is more likely
to understand.

00:03:13.769 --> 00:03:19.760
Unfortunately, the Hebrew translation of Shrek
2 contained a joke so controversial that the

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:22.560
film was banned in Israel until the line was
removed.

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:27.159
In the original English-language version,
a character threatens to castrate Puss in

00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:27.660
Boots.

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:28.760
"Neuter him right here.

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:29.920
Give him the Bob Barker treatment!"

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:35.060
Suspecting that Israeli audiences wouldn't
get the reference, the translation team changed

00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:37.980
the line to "Let's do a David D'Or on him."

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:43.760
D'Or is an Israeli singer known for his high
voice, but he didn't find the joke funny,

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:48.559
and he successfully managed to get a court
order issued that banned the film from theaters

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:50.680
until the offending line was removed.

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:52.560
The Hunger Games

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:57.959
There's a very fine line between tasteful
symbolism and straight-up gore when it comes

00:03:57.960 --> 00:03:59.660
to depicting violence on screen.

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:04.340
While there isn't an objective answer to where
that boundary lies, it's true that the US

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:07.840
tends to accept violence in movies more readily
than other countries.

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:11.900
Vietnam, for instance, banned The Hunger Games
for its violent imagery.

00:04:11.909 --> 00:04:16.130
The film's Vietnamese premiere was delayed
before it was ultimately banned altogether.

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:18.160
According to the country's National Film Board,

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:23.880
"The bloody scenes may be acceptable in America,
but in Vietnam, they're too violent and ruthless."

00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:28.960
Despite the ban, some Vietnamese fans announced
they planned to see the film in nearby Cambodia

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:29.920
or Singapore.

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:32.040
Borat and Brüno

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:37.400
Some films are so willfully controversial
that it's no surprise when they get banned.

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:45.220
Sacha Baron Cohen might be an expert on that,
as 2006's Borat and 2009's Bruno both prompted

00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:46.460
outcry worldwide.

00:04:46.750 --> 00:04:51.540
Each film follows outlandish characters in
outrageous, often offensive scenarios.

00:04:51.550 --> 00:04:57.000
Borat was unsurprisingly banned from Kazakhstan
for its offensive portrayal of the country.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:59.560
"Over here, our town kindergarten."

00:05:00.710 --> 00:05:04.400
It was also banned in Russia as well as multiple
Arab countries.

00:05:04.420 --> 00:05:07.100
Bruno fared slightly better, but not by much.

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:11.560
Ukraine banned it for its highly explicit
content, while Malaysia banned it for pretty

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:12.560
much the same reason.

00:05:12.680 --> 00:05:17.320
Despite the bans, both films managed to gross
many hundreds of millions worldwide.

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:18.880
Deadpool

00:05:19.580 --> 00:05:25.540
In 2016, Deadpool became the highest-grossing
R-rated movie of all time worldwide, despite

00:05:25.550 --> 00:05:30.860
a pretty significant handicap: it was denied
release in China due to its extreme levels

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:31.640
of violence.

00:05:31.660 --> 00:05:36.419
The film was deemed to be so constantly violent
that there wasn't any way to censor it while

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:37.480
retaining the plot.

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:42.040
While it's impressive that Deadpool made so
much without any Chinese revenue, it's a real

00:05:42.040 --> 00:05:46.580
shame for Chinese fans who weren't able to
see the Merc with a Mouth on the big screen.

00:05:46.700 --> 00:05:47.780
"Maximum effort."

00:05:52.600 --> 00:05:57.880
Luckily for a few fortunate filmgoers, Deadpool
eventually did premiere in China, although

00:05:57.889 --> 00:06:03.130
not as a full release, as it screened at the
2018 International Beijing Film Festival.

